# Chess Engine Development Rules

## Project Structure & Organization

### Type Definitions

- **ALWAYS** define types in separate files under `src/types/`
- Use descriptive interface names (e.g., `CoachingMessage`, `GameState`)
- Export types from dedicated files, never inline in components
- Group related types together (e.g., `coaching.ts`, `chess-engine.ts`)

### File Organization

```
src/
├── types/           # All TypeScript type definitions
├── components/      # React components
├── lib/            # Business logic and utilities
├── contexts/       # React contexts
└── styles/         # CSS and styling files
```

## Code Quality Standards

### TypeScript Best Practices

- Use strict typing - avoid `any` types
- Define proper interfaces for all props and state
- Use union types for limited options (e.g., `"w" | "b"`)
- Always check for undefined/null before accessing properties
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`)

### React Component Guidelines

- Use functional components with hooks
- Define prop interfaces separately in types files
- Use proper key props for list items
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use `useCallback` and `useMemo` for performance optimization

### Chess Engine Integration

- Always validate FEN strings before processing
- Handle Stockfish worker communication properly
- Implement proper error handling for engine failures
- Use consistent evaluation units (centipawns)
- Log important events but avoid console spam

## AI Coaching System

### Coaching Toaster Requirements

- Must integrate with actual game state from ChessContext
- Should receive real evaluations from Stockfish engine
- Must provide meaningful feedback based on move quality
- Should be draggable and resizable
- Must have proper show/hide animations

### Coaching Logic

- Analyze moves based on evaluation changes
- Provide different message types (blunder, brilliant, etc.)
- Give contextual advice based on game phase
- Store coaching history for learning patterns

## Stockfish Engine Integration

### UCI Options

- Always set Skill Level to 20 for maximum strength
- Use proper hash table size (64MB)
- Disable UCI_LimitStrength for full evaluation
- Set appropriate time limits for analysis

### Evaluation System

- Use enhanced material evaluation when Stockfish returns 0 cp
- Provide fallback evaluation for positions with material differences
- Log evaluation changes for debugging
- Handle mate scores properly

## Error Handling

### Required Error Handling

- Stockfish worker failures
- Invalid FEN strings
- Network connectivity issues
- Component mounting/unmounting edge cases
- User input validation

### Logging Standards

- Use consistent log prefixes (e.g., `🔧 StockfishEngine:`)
- Log important state changes
- Avoid excessive debug logging in production
- Use structured logging for analytics

## Performance Considerations

### Optimization Rules

- Lazy load heavy components
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Implement proper cleanup in useEffect
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Use Web Workers for heavy computations

### Memory Management

- Clean up event listeners
- Cancel pending requests on unmount
- Clear intervals and timeouts
- Dispose of Web Workers properly

## Testing Requirements

### Component Testing

- Test user interactions
- Verify prop passing
- Test error states
- Validate accessibility

### Integration Testing

- Test Stockfish communication
- Verify coaching system integration
- Test game state synchronization
- Validate evaluation accuracy

## Accessibility & UX

### User Experience

- Provide clear visual feedback
- Use consistent color schemes
- Implement proper loading states
- Handle edge cases gracefully

### Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Provide proper ARIA labels
- Ensure keyboard navigation
- Support screen readers

## Security & Best Practices

### Security

- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize data before processing
- Use proper CORS settings
- Implement rate limiting for API calls

### Code Quality

- Use ESLint and Prettier
- Follow consistent naming conventions
- Write self-documenting code
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

### Type Safety Issues

- Don't use `any` types
- Always check for undefined properties
- Use proper type guards
- Validate external data

### React Anti-patterns

- Don't mutate state directly
- Avoid unnecessary useEffect dependencies
- Don't forget cleanup functions
- Use proper key props

### Chess Engine Issues

- Don't ignore Stockfish errors
- Always validate FEN strings
- Handle timeout scenarios
- Don't block UI with engine calls

## Development Workflow

### Before Making Changes

1. Check existing types in `src/types/`
2. Verify component interfaces
3. Plan error handling strategy
4. Consider performance implications

### During Development

1. Use proper TypeScript types
2. Implement comprehensive error handling
3. Add appropriate logging
4. Test edge cases

### After Implementation

1. Run linting and type checking
2. Test all user interactions
3. Verify integration points
4. Update documentation if needed

## AI Agent Collaboration Guidelines

### When Working with AI Agents

- Always reference existing types before creating new ones
- Check for similar implementations before duplicating code
- Use consistent naming patterns across the codebase
- Follow the established file organization structure
- Implement proper error handling from the start
- Test integration points thoroughly

### Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Types are properly defined in separate files
- [ ] Components use proper prop interfaces
- [ ] Error handling is comprehensive
- [ ] Performance considerations are addressed
- [ ] Accessibility requirements are met
- [ ] Integration points are tested
- [ ] Logging is appropriate and consistent
